WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.530
A man shouts in anger, then says, "It was out of my hands." But could he really have done otherwise in that moment — or is that sentence a confession that holds for every choice in our lives?

2
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.300
If you did not choose everything that made you who you are, then who is doing the choosing?

3
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.150
Whose hand is it that silences the alarm at the crack of dawn?

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.400
You reach out, you touch the screen, you decide to sleep another ten minutes.

5
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:44.210
Because of those ten minutes you miss the bus, you are late for work, and as you come through the door you say, "it was out of my hands." There was traffic, the alarm never rang, I couldn't wake up.

6
00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:49.300
Behind the sentence sits a quiet defence: I didn't do it, it happened to me.

7
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:56.310
In Turkish that sentence has a very close relative: kaderimde varmış, it was written in my fate.

8
00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.440
Two words, and yet the weight they carry is surprisingly heavy.

9
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.450
Sometimes they genuinely console.

10
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:13.820
Spoken after a relative who never came off the operating table, "it was written in my fate" lifts from a person's shoulders a burden they could not carry and silences a question they could not answer.

11
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.470
And sometimes they do exactly the opposite.

12
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:25.440
When a father who has raised his hand against his own child for years says the same words, they are no longer consolation but escape.

13
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:32.770
The same two words: in one case a cloth laid gently over a wound, in the other a curtain drawn across a crime.

14
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.460
Where is the difference?

15
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.630
The difference lies in the raising of the hand.

16
00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.400
The hand that determined the outcome on the operating table was not your hand.

17
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:52.490
But the hand that struck the child was yours; you lifted that arm, you tightened those muscles, in that moment you could have stopped and you did not.

18
00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.340
Our intuition here is very sharp.

19
00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:02.190
We blame nobody for an earthquake, yet we do not let the contractor who poured rotten concrete call the earthquake fate.

20
00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:10.960
In other words, in daily life all of us, without noticing, draw an extremely fine line: a border between what happens to us and what we do.

21
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.690
The trouble is that this border, once you think about it for a while, begins to melt in your hands.

22
00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.740
Try it like this.

23
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.510
Suppose that at breakfast this morning you chose the olives rather than the cheese.

24
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.560
Why?

25
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.770
You felt like olives.

26
00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.780
And did you choose to feel like olives?

27
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.470
No, that craving came to you.

28
00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.520
Where did it come from?

29
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:45.890
Perhaps from not having eaten anything salty the night before, perhaps from having eaten olives every morning as a child, perhaps because your blood sugar sat at a particular level that morning.

30
00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.260
You built none of these.

31
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.510
If you did not choose the desire behind the choice, is the choice really yours?

32
00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.720
Or ask a harder version.

33
00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.570
Picture a man who cannot hold his anger.

34
00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:03.340
He shouts, he slams the door, and then he is full of regret.

35
00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:12.430
This man watched an angry father; he grew up being shouted at; the way his nervous system answers a threat took shape before he had even learned the words.

36
00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:21.520
Now, at 35, having slammed the door, he asks himself, "why am I like this?" Is the answer really "you chose to be"?

37
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.730
And at this point two questions arriving from two very different places end up, strangely, at the same door.

38
00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:39.540
One comes from the mosque, from the church, from the books of theology: if there is a God who knows everything and creates everything, and what I will do tomorrow is already settled, how can I possibly be free?

39
00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:50.230
The other comes from the laboratory, from physics textbooks, from brain scanners: if my brain is also a piece of matter, and every motion of matter is bound to physical law,

40
00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.680
then what is my decision other than the chemistry of neurons?

41
00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.650
Two questions, one shared fear.

42
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.260
The fear is this: perhaps there is no decider called "I" at all.

43
00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:13.150
Perhaps there is only a chain of events, determined from beginning to end, which merely looks like a decision from the outside, and we are spectators standing in the middle of that chain,

44
00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.440
looking at our own link and saying, "there, I chose this."

45
00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.250
This is one of philosophy's oldest and most stubborn knots.

46
00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:25.220
And notice, it is not an entirely abstract knot.

47
00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.510
A judge passing sentence in a courtroom has answered this question.

48
00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:33.640
A mother scolding her child has answered this question.

49
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:44.650
Someone trying to come out of addiction wrestles with it every morning as they say, "I won't drink today." All of us, hundreds of times a day, build a life on top of a question whose answer we do not know.

50
00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:48.300
Now let us look at how strong that chain really is.

51
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.590
Because the other side has a genuinely serious argument in hand.

52
00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:58.000
In 1674 Spinoza sent a letter to a doctor who had written to him.

53
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:05.010
Inside it was a small thought experiment, and that experiment has refused to let anyone go for 350 years.

54
00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:08.660
Throw a stone into the air, Spinoza said.

55
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.350
The stone is flying.

56
00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.960
Now imagine for a moment that the stone could think, that it had consciousness.

57
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.490
What would the stone think?

58
00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:21.980
Most likely this: "I am flying.

59
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:32.190
I want to keep flying, this is my desire, this is my effort." The stone is aware of its own motion, but it is not aware of the hand that set that motion going.

60
00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:38.720
It does not see the arm you swung, it does not know the angle you calculated, it takes no account of gravity.

61
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.650
It feels only the impulse inside it, and it calls that impulse "will."

62
00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:48.260
What Spinoza meant was merciless: the human being is that stone too.

63
00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:53.030
We are aware of our desires, but not of the causes that give birth to them.

64
00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.640
The thing we call freedom may be nothing more than a feeling produced by ignorance.

65
00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:02.890
140 years later this thought was translated into mathematics.

66
00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:12.460
The French mathematician Laplace, in his book published in 1814, A Philosophical Essay on Probabilities, imagined an intellect.

67
00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:20.430
An intellect that knows the position and velocity of every particle in the universe at a given instant, and grasps the laws of physics without a gap.

68
00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:25.680
For such an intellect, Laplace said, the future would be as open as the past.

69
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.890
No uncertainty would remain.

70
00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:32.980
What you will be thinking tomorrow at noon was already written in the state of the universe today.

71
00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:40.630
This picture is known in short as Laplace's demon, and its power to disturb lies precisely here: the demon is not a God.

72
00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:44.280
It contains nothing supernatural.

73
00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.970
There is only matter and law.

74
00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:57.020
Which is to say that removing God from the equation is not enough to erase fate; a perfect clock takes his place, and the clock binds you at least as tightly as fate did.

75
00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.710
Then the question reached the brain.

76
00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:06.080
In the early 1980s the American neurophysiologist Benjamin Libet set up a simple experiment.

77
00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:10.050
He asked his subjects to move a finger whenever they felt like it.

78
00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:24.260
He was recording three things at once: the moment the muscle moved, the moment the electrical readiness signal began in the brain, and the moment the subject said, "there, now I decided." The result was this.

79
00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:30.150
The readiness signal in the brain started roughly 550 milliseconds before the movement.

80
00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.560
But the subject's awareness of their own decision came only 200 milliseconds before it.

81
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:40.650
The 300 milliseconds in between turned into an earthquake in philosophy.

82
00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:47.020
Because what it appeared to show was this: the brain had already set out before you felt yourself decide.

83
00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:53.230
The decision was not beginning in you; you were merely receiving the news of something already begun.

84
00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:57.200
You were not the owner of your own hand but its correspondent.

85
00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:06.290
Libet's experiment is still debated today, his method of measurement has been seriously criticised, and later studies made the picture considerably more complicated.

86
00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:11.700
But once that unsettling image has entered a mind, it does not leave easily.

87
00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.510
Might the uncertainty of modern physics not open a door, though?

88
00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:20.280
We know that in the subatomic world events are not definite but probabilistic.

89
00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.050
If the chain is not as tight as we assumed, doesn't that make room for freedom?

90
00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.740
Unfortunately not.

91
00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.390
Because the thing we call freedom is not randomness.

92
00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:37.920
If your decision is settled by a particle in your brain throwing dice, that decision is not yours in the slightest.

93
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.690
You have escaped the chain only to sit down at the gambling table, and nothing more.

94
00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:49.700
Between a necessity you do not own and a coincidence you do not own, from the standpoint of "you" there is no difference.

95
00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:57.670
And this needs to be seen: the argument did not arrive in Islamic thought in the modern age; it was one of the oldest quarrels there.

96
00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:05.160
The line attributed to Jahm ibn Safwan, who lived in the first half of the eighth century under the Umayyads, is called Jabriyya.

97
00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:12.810
For the Jabriyya the human being is like a leaf tossed on the wind; the act is ascribed to him, but in reality he is not its owner.

98
00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:24.780
At the opposite extreme stood the line associated with names like Ma'bad al-Juhani and Ghaylan al-Dimashqi in the late seventh century, later developed by the Mu'tazila: the human being is the creator of his own act,

99
00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.110
otherwise punishing him would not be justice.

100
00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:32.720
Both extremes looked consistent, and both extremes lost something.

101
00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.930
In Jabriyya, responsibility disappeared, because you cannot put a leaf on trial for being blown about.

102
00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:45.460
In the Mu'tazila line, according to its critics, creators independent of God appeared in the universe.

103
00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:51.990
And here, right in the middle of these two abysses, one of the subtlest concepts in the history of thought was born.

104
00:09:51.990 --> 00:10:00.600
The story goes that in Basra a man in his forties climbed the pulpit and announced that he was parting from the Mu'tazila view he had defended for years.

105
00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:10.970
His name was Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari; he was born in 873, died in 936, and left behind one of the main arteries of Islamic thought.

106
00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:14.780
Ash'ari's solution fits into a single word: kasb.

107
00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:19.550
In the dictionary it means to earn, to acquire.

108
00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:28.480
The idea is this: it is God who creates the act, but it is the human being who acquires that act, takes it on, makes it his own.

109
00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.490
Creating and acquiring are not the same thing.

110
00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:35.620
You do not create the water you drink, but you are the one drinking.

111
00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:42.790
The power for the work you do is given to you from outside, yet the direction in which you turn that power is written to your account.

112
00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:46.760
This solution has been criticised since the day it was born.

113
00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:51.530
Its opponents said that kasb is not an answer but a name placed where the answer should be.

114
00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:57.260
If God creates the act in its entirety, what exactly is the human being's "acquiring"?

115
00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:01.390
A change of label, or a real share?

116
00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:05.840
Another geography tried to answer that question a little more sharply.

117
00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:19.090
In Samarkand, in the territory of present-day Uzbekistan, Abu Mansur al-Maturidi, who died in 944, drew the distinction more clearly in his Kitab al-Tawhid, the Book of Divine Unity.

118
00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:24.180
According to Maturidi and the tradition that followed him, there are two layers in an act.

119
00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:29.270
The act's coming into existence, its actual appearance, is by God's creation.

120
00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:37.720
But the inclination that turns towards that act, that hair-thin moment of preference between two options, belongs to the human being himself.

121
00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:48.730
Later Maturidi scholars called this juz'i irada, particular will: over against the universal will, that is, general will and power, a small, limited preference given to the human being,

122
00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.420
yet one that genuinely belongs to him.

123
00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.150
Think of it this way.

124
00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.640
You do not generate the electricity in your home.

125
00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:04.290
You did not build the grid, you did not create the current, the source of the power that arrives when you press the switch lies outside you.

126
00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.140
But you are the one pressing the switch.

127
00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:12.550
With that same current you can light a room, and with that same current you can set something on fire.

128
00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:16.520
The power is not yours; the direction is.

129
00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:23.370
If you understand particular will in this way, it becomes clear why the concept is at once so small and so important.

130
00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:28.300
It is small, because it does not grant you the authority to create the world.

131
00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:37.390
You do not determine how many years you will live, which family you will be born into, which illness will knock at your door, which country a war will break out in.

132
00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:39.920
None of that is in anyone's hands.

133
00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:47.090
But it is important, because what is yours is exactly that narrow gap: what you will do with the power you have been handed.

134
00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:55.380
There is one more principle the tradition of kalam used in untying this knot, in a very compact sentence: knowledge follows the known.

135
00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:00.950
That is, knowledge depends on the thing known; the thing known does not depend on knowledge.

136
00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:05.880
The astronomer who calculates when an eclipse will occur does not cause the eclipse.

137
00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:12.250
His knowledge is correct because the event will happen that way; the event does not happen that way because he knows it.

138
00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:20.060
God's eternal knowledge, according to this principle, is likewise not a command compelling the act but a flawless knowing of how the act will come about.

139
00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.750
To be known is not to be compelled.

140
00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:26.560
There is a scene that shows how practical this distinction is.

141
00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.170
During his caliphate, Umar had set out towards Damascus.

142
00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:36.420
On the road news came that there was a plague epidemic in the region he was heading for.

143
00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.630
Umar decided to turn back.

144
00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:48.840
It is related that Abu Ubayda, then at the head of the army, said to him, as if in reproach, "are you fleeing from God's decree?" and that Umar answered, "yes,

145
00:13:48.840 --> 00:14:01.450
we are fleeing from God's decree to God's decree." Then he unfolded the example: if a man brought his camels down into a valley with two slopes, one fertile and one barren, whichever slope he grazes them on,

146
00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:04.620
has he not grazed them by God's decree?

147
00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:12.750
The subtlety of that answer is here: fate is not a wall standing across from choice, but a whole within which choice itself is included.

148
00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:17.680
Taking precautions is not a rebellion outside fate; it is a part of fate.

149
00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.650
Fleeing the epidemic is fate, and walking into it is fate.

150
00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:24.340
The difference is in which one you choose.

151
00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:31.990
What is interesting is that Western thought, centuries later, arrived at a very similar place in an entirely different language.

152
00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:39.480
The line known today as compatibilism accepts that determinism may well be true, but says that freedom does not contradict it.

153
00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:47.290
On this view freedom is not being uncaused; it is being able to act according to your own wanting, without being forced from outside.

154
00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:52.060
Harry Frankfurt sharpened this with the idea of desire on two levels.

155
00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:57.630
An addict wants the substance; but at the same time he knows that he does not want to want it.

156
00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:08.000
What separates the human being from the animal and from the machine is precisely this: the ability to look at one's own desires from an upper floor and either endorse them or refuse them.

157
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.450
To be free is not to want nothing; it is to want what you want.

158
00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:22.980
Kasb and compatibilism are not the same thing, of course; one sets out from a metaphysical relation between God and the human being, the other from the laws of physics and human psychology.

159
00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:31.110
But both make the same move: they do not try to lift the human being out of the chain, they look for a genuine role for him inside it.

160
00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:35.400
Now let us come to what all of this is good for in an actual life.

161
00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:39.530
Because an idea that never enters the kitchen is only an ornament.

162
00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:45.740
Aristotle, discussing drunkenness in the Nicomachean Ethics, touches on a very practical point.

163
00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:51.150
Lawgivers, he says, punish twice over the man who commits a crime while drunk.

164
00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:55.440
At first glance that looks unfair: the man was not himself.

165
00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.930
But Aristotle's answer is shockingly solid.

166
00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:04.660
He may not have been himself while committing the crime; but he was himself when he chose to get drunk.

167
00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:10.550
Responsibility looks not at the instant the act was done, but at the road leading to that instant.

168
00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:15.160
Aristotle carries the thought all the way to character.

169
00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.330
Character is born of repeated actions.

170
00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:24.860
You do not act generously because you are generous; you become generous by acting generously, again and again.

171
00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:29.470
Which means that today's disposition is yesterday's choices, hardened.

172
00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:35.840
The bad side of this is that beyond a certain point the stone sets, and habit does not bend easily.

173
00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:39.810
The good side is that the stone is still a stone in flight.

174
00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:43.620
Today too, you are repeating something.

175
00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:47.910
And what you repeat today is the material of tomorrow's character.

176
00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.240
Something very concrete follows from this.

177
00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:54.410
Remember that man who cannot hold his anger.

178
00:16:54.410 --> 00:17:01.740
Telling him "use your will, don't get angry" is useless, because the arrival of the anger really is not in his hands.

179
00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:14.510
But if he repeats, hundreds of times, leaving the room when the anger comes, waiting ten seconds, taking a breath before forming the sentence, then six months later, even if his anger still arrives at the same speed,

180
00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.680
his hand will not rise at the same speed.

181
00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:22.450
What changes is not the emotion but the distance between the emotion and the hand.

182
00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:28.660
Particular will does not operate at the birth of the anger; it operates right there, in that narrow gap.

183
00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:32.950
The strange thing is that exactly this came out of Libet's experiment too.

184
00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:43.960
Libet showed that movement begins before consciousness; but he also saw that once consciousness enters the picture, in that final instant before the movement is carried out, his subjects could stop it.

185
00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:48.570
This was later given a name: not free will, but free veto.

186
00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:55.260
Perhaps you are not the one who starts the action; but you are the one who steps in front of the started action at the last moment.

187
00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:59.230
The hand that reaches for the cigarette reaches on its own.

188
00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:05.280
What halts it halfway is something else, and everyone who has ever tried to quit knows what that something is.

189
00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:11.330
For this reason responsibility is not something that can be suspended until the philosophical argument is over.

190
00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:17.860
The English philosopher Peter Strawson said precisely this in a famous essay he wrote in 1962.

191
00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:27.750
Even if determinism turns out to be true, he said, we cannot give up feeling gratitude towards one another, feeling resentment, forgiving, getting angry.

192
00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:34.760
Because these are not conclusions derived from a philosophical theory; they are the very substance of human relationship.

193
00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:43.210
If you stop feeling the difference between someone who pushes you deliberately and someone who bumps into you by accident in a crowd, you have stopped living among people.

194
00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:49.580
Among Turkish thinkers, one of the names who placed this matter at the centre of the will was Nurettin Topçu.

195
00:18:49.580 --> 00:19:01.230
In the doctoral work he completed at the Sorbonne in 1934, he took up the tension between conforming and revolting, and thought of the human being as a creature that constitutes itself through its action.

196
00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:08.880
For Topçu, will was not a decision taken inside the head and left there; unless it turned into movement it did not count as will.

197
00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:14.930
Morality began not in thought, but in the moment the hand rises or does not rise.

198
00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:20.020
And perhaps this is where the matter knots itself, in one respect at least.

199
00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:28.790
We expect the question "is it you who raises your hand, or is it fate?" to be closed definitively by a laboratory measurement or a theological debate.

200
00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:34.520
Yet the human being is a creature obliged to live without knowing the answer to that question.

201
00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:37.530
You do not know what you will do tomorrow.

202
00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:40.700
Because you do not know, you have to choose.

203
00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:49.630
And after you have chosen, you can argue about whether that choice was settled in advance; but at the moment of choosing, what you hold is not an argument, only that moment.

204
00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:55.200
Perhaps fate is not a sealed envelope placed before you, but a terrain you are walking through.

205
00:19:55.200 --> 00:20:00.770
You did not put the mountain there, you did not carve the river, you are not the one warming the air.

206
00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:04.740
You did not choose where you were born or how far you will walk.

207
00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:14.790
But which of the two slopes you turn towards, whether you reach out your hand to someone who has fallen, whether you raise your arm when the anger comes: that is exactly where you are standing.

208
00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:25.800
And when that moment arrives, it is worth looking once more before saying "it was out of my hands." Because most of the time, the one thing that is in your hands is the hand itself.

209
00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:29.770
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

210
00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:33.420
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
